\textbf{\textcolor{red}{AIMS OF TIME SERIES ANALYSIS}}

$\to$ \textbf{Describe} -- trend, seasonal, cycle (Often, to de-trend, de-seasonalize). If multivariate: find common patterns, clusters, dim. reductions etc... 

$\to$ \textbf{Explain} 
- modeling and inference write a model and estimate its parameters
- \textbf{Forecast} accounting for uncertainty

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{FITTING A TREND}}

For $Y_t = T_t + E_t$ (no seasonal comp.):

\textbf{1. OLS.} 
\textbf{2. Moving averages.} e.g.\ MA of order $k=3$:
\[ \widehat{T}_t = \frac{y_{t-1} + y_t + y_{t+1}}{3}, t = 2, 3, \dots \]
If $k$ is even, e.g.\ $k = 4$:
\[ \widehat{T}_t = \frac{0.5\, y_{t-2} + y_{t-1} + y_t + y_{t+1} + 0.5\, y_{t+2}}{4}. \]

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{DETRENDING}}
\[ y_t^{\text{detrended}} = y_t - \widehat{T}_t. \]

after you detrend, you remove the seasonal coponent

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{STATIONARITY}}

\begin{keydef}
\textbf{Weak (2nd-order) stationarity.} A time series $(Y_t)$ is \textbf{stationary} (2nd-order / weak) if $E(Y_t)$ and $E(Y_t Y_{t+h})$ exist, are finite, and do not depend on $t$, for every $h = 1, 2, \dots$
\[ \mu(t) = \mu \quad \text{(constant mean)}, \qquad \sigma^2(t) = \sigma^2 \quad \text{(constant variance)}, \qquad \gamma(t, t+h) = \tilde\gamma(h) \quad \text{(autocov.\ depends on lag } h \text{ only)}. \]
\end{keydef}

Naive: the \textcolor{red}{TIME SERIES} is a sequence of obs taken over time (see fig) \[ y_{1:T} = (y_1, \dots, y_T) \]
\begin{keydef}
\textbf{A time series} is a stochastic process $(Y_t)_{t \in T }$ with $Y_t \in Y$. The data $(y1, . . . , yt)$ are then regarded as (the initial trait of <- finite) a realization of the stochastic process. 
\end{keydef} 


\begin{keydef}
\textbf{A stochastic process} $(Yt)_{t \in T}$ is a sequence of random vectors (a multidimensional random variable) indexed by t, with t in an index set T . 
\end{keydef} 

Note:
- This is not a random variable, nor a random vector. It is a sequence of random variables\vectors! So eg the p. of a repeated game.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{EXAMPLE 2 — WHITE NOISE}}

A \textbf{white noise} is a process $(Y_t)_{t \ge 1}$ such that the $Y_t$ are iid and:\[ \mathbb{E}[Y_t] = \mu, \qquad \mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = \sigma^2, \qquad \gamma(t, t') = \mathrm{Cov}(Y_t, Y_{t'}) = 0 \ \text{for } t \neq t'. \]
\textbf{Gaussian white noise:} additionally require $Y_t$ iid Gaussian:\[ Y_t \stackrel{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(\mu, \sigma^2). \]Under the Gaussian assumption, uncorrelated $\Longleftrightarrow$ independent, so the distinction in the first bullet disappears.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{BAYESIAN vs FREQUENTIST INFERENCE}}

After deriving Bayes' rule and seeing the prior get \emph{updated} by evidence into a posterior, a natural question arises: \emph{does the prior shift mean there is no true $\theta$?} The answer is no --- but to see why, we need to compare the two frameworks side by side.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{The status of $\theta$.}}

$\bullet$ \emph{Frequentist.} $\theta$ is a \textbf{fixed unknown constant} of nature. It does not have a probability distribution. Probability is reserved for the data. Not a random variable. Ucertainty is driven by the samplig procedure (we see a different estimate just because the sample is not fully representative of the population)"$P(\hat\theta \in [a, b]) = 0.95$" means: if we repeated the sampling many times, 95\% of the resulting intervals $[\hat a_n, \hat b_n]$ would contain the true $\theta$.


$\bullet$ \emph{Bayesian.} $\theta$ may also be a fixed unknown constant in nature --- but since we don't \emph{know} it, we represent our \textbf{state of belief} about $\theta$ as a probability distribution: $\pi(\theta)$ before data, $p(\theta \mid y)$ after. The randomness lives in our \emph{knowledge} (is epistemic), not in $\theta$ itself. $\theta$ is a **random variable.** 

So the prior shifting does \emph{not} mean $\theta$ moves. It means our \emph{uncertainty about $\theta$} sharpens around the values most compatible with the data. The true $\theta$ (if you believe in one) is a fixed point in parameter space; the probability mass representing our knowledge is what slides around it.


\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Confidence intervals (frequentist) vs Credible intervals (Bayesian).}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Frequentist confidence interval --- exact formula.}} Given an estimator $\hat\theta_n$ with (asymptotic) standard error $\text{SE}(\hat\theta_n)$ (how the estimator estimates would vary across replicaitons of the experiment, derived analytically from the model), the $1 - \alpha$ Wald confidence interval is
\[ \text{CI}_{1-\alpha} \;=\; \bigl[\, \hat\theta_n - z_{\alpha/2} \cdot \text{SE}(\hat\theta_n),\;\; \hat\theta_n + z_{\alpha/2} \cdot \text{SE}(\hat\theta_n) \,\bigr], \]
where $\text{SE}(\hat\theta_n) = \sigma / \sqrt{n}$., and $z_{\alpha/2}$ is the $1 - \alpha/2$ quantile of the standard Normal (e.g.\ $z_{0.025} = 1.96$ for 95\%).





\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Bayesian credible interval --- exact formula.}} A $1 - \alpha$ credible interval is any interval $[a, b]$ such that the posterior probability
\[ P(\, a \le \theta \le b \mid y \,) \;=\; \int_{a}^{b} p(\theta \mid y)\, d\theta \;=\; 1 - \alpha. \]
Now $y$ is fixed (the observed data) and $\theta$ is the random variable; the probability is over the posterior $p(\theta \mid y)$.



\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Side-by-side summary.}}
\begin{tabular}{l|l|l} & Frequentist & Bayesian \\ \hline Status of $\theta$ & fixed unknown constant & random variable (uncertainty over) \\ Source of randomness & data only & data + parameter \\ Estimator of $\theta$ & MLE $\hat\theta$ & posterior $p(\theta \mid y)$ \\ Interval & confidence (over procedure) & credible (over $\theta$) \\ Updating with new data & re-run estimator & posterior $\to$ new prior, apply Bayes \end{tabular}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{EXAMPLE SSMs --- HUB: template, worked 2x2 example, map.}}


\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Canonical DLM template.}} Every Part (B) in the linked snippets is a \emph{specialisation} of:
\begin{keydef}
\textbf{General DLM (linear Gaussian SSM).}

\emph{State equation:} $\;\theta_t \;=\; G_t\, \theta_{t-1} \;+\; w_t,\qquad w_t \stackrel{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0,\, W_t),\quad \theta_t \in \mathbb{R}^p.$

\emph{Observation equation:} $\;Y_t \;=\; F_t\, \theta_t \;+\; v_t,\qquad v_t \stackrel{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0,\, V_t),\quad Y_t \in \mathbb{R}^q.$

\emph{Independence:} $\{w_t\} \perp \{v_s\}$ and both $\perp\ \theta_0 \sim \mathcal{N}(m_0, C_0)$.
\end{keydef}
Each example picks $(G, F, W, V)$ (often time-invariant). Optional additive inputs $u_t$ (constant drift, exogenous regressors) extend the state eq.\ to $\theta_t = G\, \theta_{t-1} + u_t + w_t$.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Concrete example --- multidimensional $G$ and $F$: trend + AR(2) cycle.}}

A truly multidimensional setup: observation $=$ trend (random walk) $+$ cycle (AR(2)) $+$ noise. 

\emph{Components.}

$\bullet$ Trend (random walk): $T_t = T_{t-1} + u_t,\quad u_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_T^2)$.

$\bullet$ Cycle (AR(2)): $C_t = \phi_1\, C_{t-1} + \phi_2\, C_{t-2} + r_t,\quad r_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_C^2)$.

$\bullet$ Observation: $Y_t = T_t + C_t + \nu_t,\quad \nu_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_\nu^2)$.

\emph{DLM specialisation.} 
\emph{Apply $F$ and add observation noise.}
From what Yt has to be equal to...
\[ Y_t \;=\; F\, \theta_t + \nu_t \;=\; (1,\; 1,\; 0) \begin{pmatrix} T_t \\ C_t \\ C_{t-1} \end{pmatrix} + \nu_t \;=\; T_t + C_t + \nu_t \qquad \text{with} \quad V = \sigma_\nu^2  \;\checkmark \]

From what $\theta_t$ has to be equal to...
\[ \theta_t \;=\; G\, \theta_{t-1} + w_t \;=\; \begin{pmatrix} T_{t-1} + u_t \\ \phi_1 C_{t-1} + \phi_2 C_{t-2} + r_t \\ C_{t-1} \end{pmatrix} \;=\; \begin{pmatrix} T_t \\ C_t \\ C_{t-1} \end{pmatrix}. \;\checkmark \]
with: 
\[ G\, \theta_{t-1} \;=\; \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & \phi_1 & \phi_2 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} T_{t-1} \\ C_{t-1} \\ C_{t-2} \end{pmatrix} \;=\; \begin{pmatrix} T_{t-1} \\ \phi_1 C_{t-1} + \phi_2 C_{t-2} \\ C_{t-1} \end{pmatrix}, \qquad w_t \;=\; \begin{pmatrix} u_t \\ r_t \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}, \qquad W \;=\; \begin{pmatrix} \sigma_T^2 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & \sigma_C^2 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \]

\emph{Remark.}
You need to carry $C_{t-1}$ forward for computing ne next period $\theta_t$



\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Specialisation tree.}}
\[ \underbrace{\text{General SSM}}_{\text{any } f(\theta_t \mid \theta_{t-1}),\, f(y_t \mid \theta_t)} \begin{cases} \xrightarrow[\text{state} = \mathbb{R}^p]{\text{linear Gaussian}} \underbrace{\text{DLM}}_{\text{template above}} & \text{(Examples 1, 2, 3, 4)} \\[6pt] \xrightarrow[\text{state} = \{1,\dots,K\}]{\text{discretise state}} \underbrace{\text{HMM}}_{S_t \mid S_{t-1} = i \sim \text{Cat}(p_{i,\cdot}),\; Y_t \mid S_t \sim f(\cdot \mid \theta^*_{S_t})} & \text{(separate snippets)} \end{cases} \]

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Summary table --- one model per column, 4-step DLM template per row.}}

\begin{tabular}{l|l|l|l|l|l|l|l|l}
 & \textbf{Global trend} & \textbf{RW + drift} & \textbf{Local-level} (RW + noise) & \textbf{LLT / Local linear growth} & \textbf{DLR} & \textbf{AR(1) deg.\ DLM} & \textbf{AR($p$) deg.\ DLM} & \textbf{HMM} \\
\hline
\emph{Idea} & Line $+$ iid noise; params $(\mu_0, \delta)$ fixed. & Wandering level $+$ constant drift; level $=$ RW(drift). & Hidden RW level observed through a noisy sensor; no drift. & Wandering level $+$ wandering slope. & Regression coefficients drift; predictor $x_t$ generic. & Direct AR(1) observation; innovation noise only. & Direct AR($p$); state augmented to $p$ lags. & Discrete-state Markov chain $+$ emission distribution. \\
\hline
\emph{Equations (4-step DLM template)} & \textbf{(1)} $\theta_t = (\mu_0, \delta)^\top \in \mathbb{R}^2$; \textbf{(2)} components frozen (no noise); \textbf{(3)} $\theta_t = I_2 \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\mu_0 \\ \delta\end{smallmatrix}\bigr) = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\mu_0 \\ \delta\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$ (frozen); $G = I_2$, $W = 0$; \textbf{(4)} $Y_t = F_t\theta_t + \varepsilon_t = \mu_0 + \delta t + \varepsilon_t$, $F_t = (1, t)$, $\varepsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$. & \textbf{(1)} $\theta_t \in \mathbb{R}^1$ (scalar level); \textbf{(2)} $\theta_t = \theta_{t-1} + \mu + w_t$, $w_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_w^2)$; \textbf{(3)} $G = 1$, drift input $\mu$; \textbf{(4)} $Y_t = \theta_t + \varepsilon_t$, $F = 1$, $\varepsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$. & \textbf{(1)} $\theta_t \in \mathbb{R}^1$ (hidden level); \textbf{(2)} $\theta_t = \theta_{t-1} + z_t$, $z_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_z^2)$; \textbf{(3)} $G = 1$, $W = \sigma_z^2$; \textbf{(4)} $Y_t = \theta_t + \varepsilon_t$, $F = 1$, $V = \sigma_\varepsilon^2$. & \textbf{(1)} $\theta_t = (\mu_t, \beta_t)^\top \in \mathbb{R}^2$ ($\mu_t$ level, $\beta_t$ slope); \textbf{(2)} $\mu_t = \mu_{t-1} + \beta_{t-1} + w_{1,t}$, $\beta_t = \beta_{t-1} + w_{2,t}$, both Gaussian; \textbf{(3)} $\theta_t = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\mu_{t-1} \\ \beta_{t-1}\end{smallmatrix}\bigr) + \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}w_{1,t} \\ w_{2,t}\end{smallmatrix}\bigr) = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\mu_{t-1}+\beta_{t-1}+w_{1,t} \\ \beta_{t-1}+w_{2,t}\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$; $G = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$, $W = \mathrm{diag}(\sigma_{w_1}^2, \sigma_{w_2}^2)$; \textbf{(4)} $Y_t = \mu_t + \varepsilon_t$, $F = (1, 0)$, $V = \sigma^2$. & \textbf{(1)} $\theta_t = (\alpha_t, \beta_t)^\top \in \mathbb{R}^2$ ($\alpha_t$ intercept, $\beta_t$ slope on $x_t$); \textbf{(2)} $\alpha_t = \alpha_{t-1} + w_{1,t}$, $\beta_t = \beta_{t-1} + w_{2,t}$; \textbf{(3)} $\theta_t = I_2 \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\alpha_{t-1} \\ \beta_{t-1}\end{smallmatrix}\bigr) + \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}w_{1,t} \\ w_{2,t}\end{smallmatrix}\bigr) = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\alpha_{t-1}+w_{1,t} \\ \beta_{t-1}+w_{2,t}\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$; $G = I_2$, $W = \mathrm{diag}(\sigma_{w_1}^2, \sigma_{w_2}^2)$; \textbf{(4)} $Y_t = \alpha_t + \beta_t x_t + \varepsilon_t$, $F_t = (1, x_t)$. & \textbf{(1)} $\theta_t \in \mathbb{R}^1$ ($= Y_t$); \textbf{(2)} $\theta_t = \varphi\, \theta_{t-1} + \varepsilon_t$; \textbf{(3)} $\theta_t = \varphi \theta_{t-1} + \varepsilon_t$ (scalar result); $G = \varphi$, $W = \sigma^2$; \textbf{(4)} $Y_t = \theta_t$, $F = 1$, $V = 0$ (degenerate). & \textbf{(1)} $\theta_t = (\theta_{1,t}, \dots, \theta_{p,t})^\top \in \mathbb{R}^p$, $\theta_{1,t} = Y_t$, others auxiliary (book form); \textbf{(2)} $\theta_{1,t} = \phi_1\theta_{1,t-1}+\theta_{2,t-1}+\varepsilon_t$; $\theta_{j,t} = \phi_j \theta_{1,t-1} + \theta_{j+1,t-1}$ ($j < p$); $\theta_{p,t} = \phi_p \theta_{1,t-1}$; \textbf{(3)} $\theta_t = G\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\theta_{1,t-1} \\ \vdots \\ \theta_{p,t-1}\end{smallmatrix}\bigr) + \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\varepsilon_t \\ 0 \\ \vdots\end{smallmatrix}\bigr) = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\phi_1\theta_{1,t-1}+\theta_{2,t-1}+\varepsilon_t \\ \phi_2\theta_{1,t-1}+\theta_{3,t-1} \\ \vdots\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$; $G$ book-companion ($\phi$'s in first column), $W = \mathrm{diag}(\sigma^2, 0, \dots, 0)$; \textbf{(4)} $Y_t = e_1^\top \theta_t$, $V = 0$. & \textbf{(1)} $S_t \in \{1, \dots, K\}$ (discrete state); \textbf{(2)} $S_t \mid S_{t-1} = i \sim \mathrm{Cat}(P_{i, \cdot})$; \textbf{(3)} not a DLM --- transition is matrix $\mathbf{P}$; \textbf{(4)} $Y_t \mid S_t = j \sim f(\cdot \mid \theta^*_j)$ (emission family). \\
\hline
\emph{Stationary? / moments} & No: $E[Y_t] = \mu_0 + \delta t$, $\mathrm{Var} = \sigma^2$ const. & No: $E[Y_t] = \theta_0 + t\mu$, $\mathrm{Var}$ grows as $t\sigma_w^2 + \sigma^2$. & No: $\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = t\sigma_z^2 + \sigma_\varepsilon^2$. & No (level integrates a wandering slope); $\mathrm{Var}$ grows $\sim t^3$. & Path-dependent on $x_{1:t}$; generally non-stat. & Yes iff $|\varphi| < 1$; $\mathrm{Var}(Y) = \sigma^2/(1-\varphi^2)$. & Yes iff all roots of $1 - \sum_k \phi_k z^k$ outside unit disc. & Yes iff $\mathbf{P}$ irreducible and aperiodic. \\
\hline
\emph{Notes / nesting} & DLR with $W = 0, x_t = t$. & LLT / Local linear growth with slope frozen at $\mu$ ($\sigma_{w_2}^2 = 0$). & Hidden state $\Rightarrow V \ne 0$. Setting $\sigma_z^2 = 0$ collapses to iid Gaussian $\sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_0, \sigma_\varepsilon^2)$. & DLR with $x_t = t$. Generalises RW + drift. & Most general single-series trend model (covariate-driven). & Innovation noise active; obs exact ($V = 0$). & Same trick as AR(1), state $p$-dim. & Non-Gaussian, discrete-state SSM; needs Viterbi for decoding. \\
\end{tabular}

\emph{Conventions.} $e_1 = (1, 0, \dots, 0)^\top$ is the first standard basis vector. Lower-case $z_t, \varepsilon_t, w_t, w_{i,t}$ are Gaussian noises. \emph{Degenerate DLM} ($V = 0$): observation equation collapses to $Y_t = F\, \theta_t$, so the "latent" state is observed exactly --- characteristic of directly-observed Markov processes (AR family).

\emph{See linked snippets for each example in full.}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{TREND DLMs --- four models, one line of thought.}}

A unified treatment of \emph{Global trend}, \emph{RW + drift}, \emph{Local linear trend (LLT / Local linear growth)}, and \emph{Dynamic linear regression (DLR)}. Each is a relaxation of the previous one: every step lets a previously-fixed quantity become stochastic.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{The chain of thought.}}

\emph{(1) Simplest possible trend.} A straight line plus noise. Slope and intercept are fixed numbers. \textbf{$\to$ Global trend.}

\emph{(2) But what if the level itself wanders?} Same average direction, but the realised level is a random walk around the line. \textbf{$\to$ RW + drift.}

\emph{(3) What if the slope also wanders?} The "global" trend becomes "local." \textbf{$\to$ LLT / Local linear growth.}

\emph{(4) What if the trend depends on an external variable, not just time?} Replace "time" as predictor with any covariate $x_t$; let coefficients drift. \textbf{$\to$ DLR.}

All four are in the canonical DLM form $\theta_t = G\, \theta_{t-1} + w_t$, $Y_t = F_t\, \theta_t + v_t$, with $w_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, W)$ and $v_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, V)$.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Side-by-side table --- one model per column.}}

\begin{tabular}{l|l|l|l|l}
& \textbf{Global trend} & \textbf{RW + drift} & \textbf{LLT / Local linear growth} & \textbf{DLR} \\
\hline
\emph{Idea / intuition} & Line $+$ iid noise; parameters fixed. & Drunkard with constant lean: average step $\mu$, staggers around it. Level wanders, drift fixed. & Trend with both wandering level and wandering slope. Captures shifting growth rates. & Regression where the $Y$-vs-$x$ relation evolves. Predictor is an arbitrary covariate, coefficients drift. \\
\hline
\emph{Equations (4-step DLM template)} & \textbf{(1)} $\theta_t = (\mu_0, \delta)^\top \in \mathbb{R}^2$, $\mu_0$ \emph{intercept}, $\delta$ \emph{slope}. \;\textbf{(2)} \emph{Component eq.:} $\mu_0 = \mu_0$, $\delta = \delta$ (frozen, no noise). \;\textbf{(3)} \emph{Matrix state eq.:} $\theta_t = G\theta_{t-1} + w_t = I_2 \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\mu_0 \\ \delta\end{smallmatrix}\bigr) + \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}0 \\ 0\end{smallmatrix}\bigr) = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\mu_0 \\ \delta\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$ (frozen, $G = I_2$, $W = 0$). \emph{Components after multiplication:} $\theta_{t,1} = \mu_0$, $\theta_{t,2} = \delta$. \;\textbf{(4)} \emph{Obs eq.:} $Y_t = F_t\, \theta_t + \varepsilon_t = \mu_0 + \delta t + \varepsilon_t$, $F_t = (1, t)$, $\varepsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$. & \textbf{(1)} $\theta_t \in \mathbb{R}^1$ (scalar level). \;\textbf{(2)} \emph{Component eq.:} $\theta_t = \theta_{t-1} + \mu + w_t$, $w_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_w^2)$. \;\textbf{(3)} \emph{Matrix state eq.:} same as (2) since scalar: $\theta_t = \theta_{t-1} + \mu + w_t$ (= the level itself). $G = 1$, deterministic drift input $\mu$. \;\textbf{(4)} \emph{Obs eq.:} $Y_t = F\, \theta_t + \varepsilon_t = \theta_t + \varepsilon_t$, $F = 1$, $\varepsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$. & \textbf{(1)} $\theta_t = (\mu_t, \beta_t)^\top \in \mathbb{R}^2$, $\mu_t$ \emph{level}, $\beta_t$ \emph{slope}. \;\textbf{(2)} \emph{Component eq.:} $\mu_t = \mu_{t-1} + \beta_{t-1} + w_{1,t}$, $w_{1,t} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_{w_1}^2)$; $\beta_t = \beta_{t-1} + w_{2,t}$, $w_{2,t} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_{w_2}^2)$. \;\textbf{(3)} \emph{Matrix state eq.:} $\theta_t = G\theta_{t-1} + w_t = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\mu_{t-1} \\ \beta_{t-1}\end{smallmatrix}\bigr) + \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}w_{1,t} \\ w_{2,t}\end{smallmatrix}\bigr) = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\mu_{t-1} + \beta_{t-1} + w_{1,t} \\ \beta_{t-1} + w_{2,t}\end{smallmatrix}\bigr) = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\mu_t \\ \beta_t\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$, $W = \mathrm{diag}(\sigma_{w_1}^2, \sigma_{w_2}^2)$. \emph{Components after multiplication:} $\mu_t = \mu_{t-1} + \beta_{t-1} + w_{1,t}$ (level absorbs slope $+$ noise); $\beta_t = \beta_{t-1} + w_{2,t}$ (slope is RW). \;\textbf{(4)} \emph{Obs eq.:} $Y_t = F\, \theta_t + \varepsilon_t = \mu_t + \varepsilon_t$, $F = (1, 0)$, $\varepsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$. & \textbf{(1)} $\theta_t = (\alpha_t, \beta_t)^\top \in \mathbb{R}^2$, $\alpha_t$ \emph{intercept}, $\beta_t$ \emph{slope on $x_t$}. \;\textbf{(2)} \emph{Component eq.:} $\alpha_t = \alpha_{t-1} + w_{1,t}$, $w_{1,t} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_{w_1}^2)$; $\beta_t = \beta_{t-1} + w_{2,t}$, $w_{2,t} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_{w_2}^2)$. \;\textbf{(3)} \emph{Matrix state eq.:} $\theta_t = G\theta_{t-1} + w_t = I_2 \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\alpha_{t-1} \\ \beta_{t-1}\end{smallmatrix}\bigr) + \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}w_{1,t} \\ w_{2,t}\end{smallmatrix}\bigr) = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\alpha_{t-1} + w_{1,t} \\ \beta_{t-1} + w_{2,t}\end{smallmatrix}\bigr) = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}\alpha_t \\ \beta_t\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$, $W = \mathrm{diag}(\sigma_{w_1}^2, \sigma_{w_2}^2)$. \emph{Components after multiplication:} $\alpha_t = \alpha_{t-1} + w_{1,t}$; $\beta_t = \beta_{t-1} + w_{2,t}$ (independent RWs). \;\textbf{(4)} \emph{Obs eq.:} $Y_t = F_t\, \theta_t + \varepsilon_t = \alpha_t + \beta_t x_t + \varepsilon_t$, $F_t = (1, x_t)$, $\varepsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$. \\
\hline
\emph{Moments / stationarity / forecasts} & $E[Y_t] = \mu_0 + \delta t$;\ \;$\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = \sigma^2$ (constant); \;forecast bands flat; \;non-stat.\ mean, constant variance. & $E[Y_t] = \theta_0 + t \mu$ (same line); \;$\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) = t \sigma_w^2 + \sigma^2$ (linear); \;forecast bands fan $\propto \sqrt{t}$; \;non-stat.\ mean and variance. & $E[Y_t] = \mu_0 + t \beta_0$ in expectation; \;$\mathrm{Var}(Y_t) \sim t^3$ asymptotic (slope integrates twice); \;forecast bands fan $\propto t^{3/2}$; \;non-stat. & $E[Y_t]$ path-dependent (on $x_{1:t}$); \;$\mathrm{Var}(Y_t)$ depends on the $x$-path; \;forecast bands depend on future $x_t$; \;generally non-stat. \\
\hline
\emph{Nesting / when to use / R} & $=$ DLR with $W = 0, x_t = t$. \;Use when residuals look iid and trend is a clean line. \;R: \verb|lm(y ~ t)|. & $=$ LLT / Local linear growth with $\sigma_{w_2}^2 = 0$. \;Use when there are long swings off the trend line. \;R: \verb|dlmModPoly(1)| with drift input. & $=$ DLR with $x_t = t$. \;Use when the growth rate itself shifts over time. \;R: \verb|dlmModPoly(2)|. & Most general in this family. \;Use when trend depends on an external variable (time-varying betas, marketing mix, clinical covariates). \;R: \verb|dlmModReg(X)|. CAPM case: yt excess return for the asset, xt = excess return for the market \\
\end{tabular}

column 1 and 2 use differet notation but you could write rw + d with a 

In LLT / Local linear growth you put $\beta$ inside $\mu_t$ and use it; in DLR you keep them separate. If I had LLT / Local linear growth-style coupling in DLR it would mean adaptive intercepts.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Each model in the other's style (same DLM, two equivalent parametrisations).}}

\emph{RW + drift, written in global-trend style (2D state, slope frozen $=$ LLT / Local linear growth with $\sigma_{w_2}^2 = 0$).}

\emph{State:} $\theta_t = G\, \theta_{t-1} + w_t$ with $\theta_t = (\mu_t,\, \beta_t)^\top$ and $\beta_t \equiv \mu$ frozen, $w_t \sim \mathcal{N}_2(0, W)$, $W = \mathrm{diag}(\sigma_w^2, 0)$, $G = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$. \;\emph{Obs:} $Y_t = F\, \theta_t + \varepsilon_t = \mu_t + \varepsilon_t$ with $\varepsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$, $F = (1,\, 0)$.

\emph{Global trend, written in RW-drift style (scalar evolving state, drift added each step, no noise).}

\emph{State:} $\theta_t = G\, \theta_{t-1} + \delta$ with $\theta_t \in \mathbb{R}$, $W = 0$, $G = 1$, deterministic drift input $\delta$. \;\emph{Obs:} $Y_t = F\, \theta_t + \varepsilon_t = \theta_t + \varepsilon_t$ with $\varepsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$, $F = 1$. \;(Unrolls to $\theta_t = \mu_0 + t\delta$, same line as the original.)

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{SUPERPOSITION PRINCIPLE --- additive component DLMs (Petris \S3.2).}}

\emph{Idea.} Build a complex DLM by \emph{adding together} $k$ simpler, mutually independent component DLMs that share one observation. Modular: pick trend + seasonal + AR + regression as bricks, stack their states, and you have a single combined DLM that the Kalman filter handles in one pass.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Setup --- $k$ independent component DLMs.}}

For each $j = 1, \dots, k$, component $j$ is its own full DLM (state $+$ obs noise) with state $\theta_{j,t} \in \mathbb{R}^{p_j}$:
\[ \theta_{j,t} = G_j\, \theta_{j,t-1} + w_{j,t}, \quad w_{j,t} \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\, W_j), \qquad Y_{j,t} = F_j\, \theta_{j,t} + v_{j,t}, \quad v_{j,t} \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\, V_j). \]
All shocks independent across components AND across time: $w_{j,t} \perp\!\!\!\perp w_{l,t}$, $v_{j,t} \perp\!\!\!\perp v_{l,t}$ for $j \ne l$, and $\theta_{j,0}$'s independent.

\emph{Observation as a sum.}
\[ Y_t \;=\; Y_{1,t} + Y_{2,t} + \cdots + Y_{k,t} \;=\; \sum_{j=1}^k F_j\, \theta_{j,t} \;+\; \underbrace{\sum_{j=1}^k v_{j,t}}_{v_t \,\sim\, \mathcal{N}(0,\, V),\; V \,=\, \sum_j V_j}. \]
\emph{Why one combined $v_t$?} The component noises $v_{j,t}$ are independent Gaussians, so their sum is Gaussian with variance $V = \sum_j V_j$. From the observed-side perspective, ``one obs noise per component'' and ``one combined obs noise $V$'' are \emph{indistinguishable} --- but the per-component split is useful for bookkeeping when each brick has its own well-understood noise scale (e.g., trend-irregular vs.\ measurement error).

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Combine --- 4-step DLM template.}}

\emph{(1) State (stack components).}
\[ \theta_t \;=\; \begin{pmatrix} \theta_{1,t} \\ \theta_{2,t} \\ \vdots \\ \theta_{k,t} \end{pmatrix} \;\in\; \mathbb{R}^{p_1 + \cdots + p_k}. \]
each $\theta_{j,t}$ can be any dimension $p_j \geq 1$

\emph{(2) Component equations.} Already given above --- each $\theta_{j,t}$ evolves on its own with $G_j, W_j$.

\emph{(3) Matrix state equation (block-diagonal $G$ and $W$).}
\[ \theta_t = G\, \theta_{t-1} + w_t, \qquad G = \mathrm{blkdiag}(G_1, \dots, G_k), \qquad W = \mathrm{blkdiag}(W_1, \dots, W_k). \]
The block-diagonal structure encodes ``no cross-coupling between components.''

\emph{(4) Observation equation (row-concatenate the $F$'s; sum the obs noises).}
\[ Y_t = F\, \theta_t + v_t, \qquad F = [\,F_1 \;\big|\; F_2 \;\big|\; \cdots \;\big|\; F_k\,], \qquad v_t = \sum_{j=1}^k v_{j,t} \;\sim\; \mathcal{N}(0,\, V),\quad V = \sum_{j=1}^k V_j. \]
\emph{Distributional form.} $\theta_t \mid \theta_{t-1} \sim \mathcal{N}\bigl(G\theta_{t-1},\, W\bigr)$; $\;Y_t \mid \theta_t \sim \mathcal{N}\bigl(F\theta_t,\, V\bigr)$ with $V = \sum_j V_j$.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Why it works (intuition).}}

$\bullet$ \emph{Gaussian closure.} A sum of independent Gaussians is Gaussian. So $\sum_j F_j \theta_{j,t}$ is Gaussian, and adding $v_t$ keeps it Gaussian.

$\bullet$ \emph{Independence preserved by stacking.} Block-diagonal $G, W$ means the components NEVER mix in mean or in shocks --- they evolve in parallel. The Kalman recursion on the combined state effectively runs $k$ parallel recursions for the conditional means, with the \emph{only} cross-talk happening through the joint update against the observation $y_t$ (which sees the sum).

$\bullet$ \emph{Modularity.} You design each brick once (LLT, seasonal, AR, regression on $x_t$) and reuse them in any combination.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Canonical example --- Basic Structural Model (BSM).}}

\[ Y_t = \underbrace{\mu_t}_{\text{trend (LLT)}} + \underbrace{\gamma_t}_{\text{seasonal}} + \underbrace{v_t}_{\text{irregular}}. \]

\emph{Trend brick (LLT, $p_1 = 2$):} $\theta_{1,t} = (\mu_t, \beta_t)$, $G_1 = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$, $F_1 = (1,\, 0)$.

\emph{Quarterly seasonal brick ($p_2 = 3$):} $\theta_{2,t} = (\gamma_t,\, \gamma_{t-1},\, \gamma_{t-2})$, $G_2 = \bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}-1 & -1 & -1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)$, $F_2 = (1, 0, 0)$.

\emph{Combined.} State $\theta_t \in \mathbb{R}^5$ (2 trend $+$ 3 seasonal), $G = \mathrm{blkdiag}(G_1, G_2)$, $F = [\,1\;0\;1\;0\;0\,]$. One DLM; one Kalman filter pass; outputs both the trend $\mu_t$ and the seasonal $\gamma_t$ separately as components of the filtered state.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{More compositions (same recipe).}}

$\bullet$ \emph{LLT + AR(1) cycle:} trend $+$ a stationary AR(1) for short-run fluctuations. State $\in \mathbb{R}^3$.

$\bullet$ \emph{LLT + regression on $x_t$:} trend $+$ DLR with time-varying $(\alpha_t, \beta_t)$. State $\in \mathbb{R}^4$. ``Trend plus covariate response.''

$\bullet$ \emph{Trend + seasonal + regression:} the classic Holt-Winters in DLM clothing.

